# tools/encoding_base32_witness.rish — Base32 (RFC 4648) authored in pure Rye: the case-safe, punctuation-free binary-to-text form a TOTP
# secret, an onion address, or a CIDv1 content id wears, proven against the RFC 4648 §10 known-answers and byte-for-byte against an
# independent u64-group implementation (encoding · the serialization sibling of Season G · Cryptography).
#
# Season: encoding — the text-form sibling of the crypto library, beside base64.rye, base58.rye, and base58check.rye. Where crypto/ authors
# the mathematics that produces bytes, encoding/ authors the text those bytes travel in, keeping the same parity discipline: authored from
# the standard, calling no std codec in the primitive, proven byte-for-byte against a reference runnable now.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (the wave that seats the Rye-first spine).
# Clean-room: the alphabet and padding law are RFC 4648's own; the arithmetic is ours (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# encoding/base32.rye authors Base32 from RFC 4648 — the A–Z 2–7 alphabet, the '=' padding law with legal counts 0/1/3/4/6, the five-byte/
# eight-symbol packing and its inverse. The primitive calls no std base32 (Zig ships none). Parity is proven at selftest against two
# references runnable now: the RFC 4648 §10 known-answers ("" · "f" · "fo" · "foo" · "foob" · "fooba" · "foobar") match text for text and
# round-trip back, and a second, INDEPENDENT u64-group implementation matches byte-for-byte across every remainder class for payloads 0..64
# bytes. A bad character refuses BadChar, a bad length or illegal pad count BadLength, a too-small destination DestTooSmall. Purely local
# arithmetic — no key, no network, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/encoding_base32_witness.rish

say "encoding-base32: Language EN — Base32 (RFC 4648) is the binary-to-text form that survives a channel harsher than Base64's: a single case, no punctuation, an alphabet (A–Z 2–7) chosen so no symbol confuses another read aloud or typed on a keypad. It is the form a TOTP secret is shared in, an onion address and a CIDv1 content id wear, a value written when it must be dictated over a voice call. The crypto library proves the bytes; this module renders them to the case-safe alphabet and back, byte-for-byte."
say "encoding-base32: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: five eight-bit bytes pack into eight five-bit symbols indexed from the 32-character alphabet; a short final group pads with '=' to a multiple of eight, the legal pad counts exactly 0, 1, 3, 4, or 6; decoding inverts the map so the same bytes always render to the same text and the same text always recovers the same bytes."
say "encoding-base32: Lens TAME — the primitive calls no std base32 (Zig ships none); it is authored from the standard, bounded input (max_decoded_bytes, max_encoded_bytes), contract asserts on every function, buffer-length checked before the first write. Parity holds against the RFC 4648 §10 known-answers AND a second, independent u64-group implementation byte-for-byte across every remainder class; a bad character refuses BadChar, a bad length or illegal pad count refuses BadLength, a too-small destination refuses DestTooSmall — each before a partial result escapes. Purely local — no key, no network, no funds, no device."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "encoding/bin/base32"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p encoding/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build encoding/base32.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "encoding-base32: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "encoding/bin/base32.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "encoding-base32: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "encoding-base32: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN encoding-base32' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "encoding-base32: selftest did not print the GREEN encoding-base32 line"
